+def derive_adaptive_max_batch(
+    *,
+    current_max_num_seqs: int,
+    max_total_num_tokens: int,
+    context_len: int,
+    reserve_divisor: int,
+    ceiling: int,
+    state_pool_capacity: int | None = None,
+) -> int:
+    """Co-derive the running-batch cap from KV-pool token capacity.
+
+    The KV page budget already gates real concurrency per decode step, so the
+    running-batch cap only needs to be a generous, graph-covered upper bound.
+    Estimate how many concurrent requests the KV pool sustains assuming each
+    holds ~``context_len / reserve_divisor`` tokens of live KV, then clamp:
+      - floor at ``current_max_num_seqs`` so the adaptive path never lowers the
+        cap (no regression vs. the static default),
+      - ceiling at ``ceiling`` so graph capture stays bounded on large HBM,
+      - round up to a multiple of 32 so the derived cap is a clean top bucket.
+
+    Mirrors SGLang's KV-derived ``max_running_requests``
+    (kv_cache_configurator.resolve_max_num_reqs) and vLLM's decoupling of the
+    graph cap from ``max_num_seqs`` (config/compilation.py:703).
+
+    Args:
+        current_max_num_seqs: the configured/static running-batch cap; acts as
+            the floor for the KV-derived value.
+        max_total_num_tokens: total KV token capacity of the device pool.
+        context_len: model context length, used to estimate live KV per request.
+        reserve_divisor: assume each request holds ``context_len /
+            reserve_divisor`` tokens of live KV.
+        ceiling: hard upper bound so graph capture stays bounded on large HBM.
+        state_pool_capacity: number of *recurrent state* slots available
+            (mamba/GDN chunk pool). Hybrid linear-attention models allocate one
+            fixed-size state chunk per running request from a pool that is
+            sized independently of the KV token pool -- and, under speculative
+            decoding, is ``1 + num_draft_tokens`` times more expensive per
+            chunk. A KV-derived cap larger than this pool lets the scheduler
